WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.709
Hey, Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.709 --> 00:00:06.360
Have you ever wondered if it’s really as
tough as everyone says it is to get into Fort

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:07.360
Knox?

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:11.440
And what makes a place truly “secure”
anyway?

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:16.369
Throughout history, mankind has worked with
nature and its own ingenuity to create some

00:00:16.369 --> 00:00:18.800
of the most fortified places on the planet.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.869
They can be found all over the world and serve
a wide range of unique and interesting purposes

00:00:23.869 --> 00:00:25.859
that you might not expect.

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:34.090
Here are nine of the most fortified places
on Earth.

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:37.750
When you think about a place so secure you’ll
never get in, what is the first thing that

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:39.290
pops into your mind?

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:42.710
Well, Fort Knox was probably the first thing
you thought of.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:48.310
That’s because “Fort Knox” is synonymous
with “a secure location” in pop culture.

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:53.370
Surprisingly, Fort Knox isn’t actually the
name of the vault where the gold is stored,

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.830
that’s the United States Bullion Depository.

00:00:56.830 --> 00:01:04.000
Fort Knox is a 109,000 acre Army post which
is home to over twelve thousand people, the

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.909
bullion vault itself, and even a high school.

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:13.200
The grounds of the bullion depository are
secured 24 hours a day by an array of CCTV

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.289
cameras, motion sensors and minefields.

00:01:16.289 --> 00:01:20.130
If you could get past all that, you would
still need to scale the surrounding electric

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:26.219
fences topped with barbed wire, get through
the granite walls, and somehow open the twenty-five-ton

00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:27.539
vault door.

00:01:27.539 --> 00:01:33.459
The vault is guarded by the United States
Mint Police, an elite force tasked with protecting

00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:37.960
assets belonging to the United States Treasury
and the US Mint.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:42.940
If that wasn’t enough, there are around
thirty thousand Army troops stationed at the

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:44.119
Fort Knox base.

00:01:44.119 --> 00:01:49.060
So if you do decide to try and take the title
of “the person that stole from Fort Knox,”

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:52.600
you better bring an army because you’ll
be fighting one.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.249
Gold bars aren’t the only treasures that
have been stored behind that reinforced door

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:57.249
though.

00:01:57.249 --> 00:02:02.950
In World War 2, both the Declaration of Independence
and the Constitution were secured there for

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:03.950
protection.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:09.979
Morphine and opium were even held there at
one point, in case the US became isolated

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:12.000
from raw sources of opium.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.970
It seems crazy that the government were hoarding
drugs in a gold vault, but until synthetic

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:22.100
painkillers were invented, opiates were the
only ones available.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:26.250
I guess they didn’t like the idea of fighting
a war without painkillers.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:31.220
Despite the legacy of Fort Knox, it isn’t
actually the largest depository of gold in

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:32.220
North America…

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:35.820
but we’ll get to that a bit later.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:39.170
Instead of gold bars, what about golden plates?

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.190
Don’t have any of them laying around?

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:45.100
Well fear not, the Mormon Church have got
you covered!

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:50.590
While not quite the golden pages of Joseph
Smith, the microfilm reels and sheets of microfiche

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:56.570
stored in The Granite Mountain Records Vault
have enough space on them to store over 4.5

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:59.500
billion pages of genealogical records.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.330
Now that’s what you call a family album!

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:08.190
The vault itself is located one mile up Little
Cottonwood Canyon, in Utah, and is built into

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:09.840
the side of the mountain.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.160
Wide loading bays allow access to the tunnels
leading into the rock face, giving way to

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.250
rooms deep inside the mountain.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:20.750
It’s in these deep, secretive recesses that
you find them.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:27.330
Hundreds upon hundreds of rows of cabinets
containing an unimaginable amount of family

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:28.330
history.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:33.210
All the rooms are climate controlled to preserve
the data, and each room is protected by heat

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:37.530
and motion sensors to detect any problems
that could lead to damage of the records.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:42.120
While it’s not as guarded as Fort Knox,
the measures which the Mormon Church have

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.940
gone through to protect and preserve these
records is simply amazing.

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:51.890
They even have a lab on site, ready to save
and reproduce any records that have begun

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:53.340
to degrade.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:57.750
The facility and its purpose are also relatively
unknown to most people.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:03.160
The church has gone to great lengths to keep
the vault as secluded and secretive as possible,

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:04.610
sorry guys.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:08.390
Photos of the site itself are extremely difficult
to find.

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:12.530
If you’re ever wondering where you came
from and ever use a genealogical database

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:17.880
to trace your ancestors, there’s a good
chance documents stored in the Mormon Church’s

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:22.180
vault played a part in tracing your history.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:27.400
Somewhere in the deep rolling hills of Pennsylvania,
there lies an innocent winding road which

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:32.510
leads up to a remarkably out-of-place chain
link fence.

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:37.460
If you keep going, you will eventually be
stopped by some serious-looking security guards

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:41.210
carrying some equally serious-looking weaponry.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:45.060
This is the point where you are either going
to be told to “take a hike” or present

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.889
your papers in order to enter the highly-secure
facility on the other side of the fence.

00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:52.930
You’ve just found Iron Mountain.

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:57.270
This is a place where most of the vault owners
are confidential, so you know there’s going

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:02.440
to be some serious security in keeping things
locked away and hidden from prying eyes.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:07.730
The facility takes advantage of a retired
limestone mine, and effectively “moved in”

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:10.570
after the rocks moved out.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:17.090
This provided a geologically secure, and cost-effective
way to house an incredibly valuable storage

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:18.210
bunker.

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:24.020
The 220 vaults contain a variety of valuables
from birth certificates to microfilm backing

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:27.060
up some extremely valuable documents.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:30.110
These are the kind of images that the world
couldn’t do without…

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:33.840
such as the original picture of Einstein sticking
his tongue out.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.690
As you wander around the facility, probably
with an escort, you will notice just how large

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:39.690
it is.

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:46.290
The whole storage area is around 100 acres,
and employs a team of 155 people working underground

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:49.070
to keep its contents maintained.

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:53.510
Deep within the facility lies a room which
emits a low hum as you approach it.

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:57.940
No… it isn’t some kind of doomsday machine
hidden along with the other secretive objects

00:05:57.940 --> 00:05:58.940
stored here.

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:03.070
The low hum comes from the energy-efficient
generators and cooling ducts that keep one

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:07.080
of the world’s most important data hives
running smoothly.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:13.320
This is the home of Room 48, a data centre
used as a backup for some of America’s biggest

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:14.320
companies.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:19.500
It isn’t the only data center humming away
in the former mine, but one of five.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:24.940
Room 48 is just the most well-known since
it has some pretty clever energy-saving tricks

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:31.090
up its sleeve, giving it a 50% power advantage
over almost every other data centre in the

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:32.090
country.

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:36.060
Whether it’s your family photos stored in
a vault, or your cloud-storage business safely

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:42.900
backed up in room 48, your assets are definitely
going to be safe in Iron Mountain.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:48.090
One place that you’d probably never want
to go to, even if you could, is the Korean

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:51.150
DMZ, or “de-militarized zone.”

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:56.300
Despite its name, it’s one of the most heavily
militarized areas on the planet.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:07:02.620
This no-man’s land stretches the 160 mile
border between North and South Korea, and

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:05.190
is 2.5 miles wide.

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:10.320
Tall razor-wire fences, which are usually
electrified, line each side of the no-man’s

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:11.320
land.

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:15.990
Listening posts, guard towers, and anti-tank
barricades are dotted across the landscape

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:17.990
as far as the eye can see.

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:23.199
To top it all off, there are hundreds of thousands
of active soldiers patrolling both sides,

00:07:23.199 --> 00:07:26.490
ready for war at any moment.

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:31.810
If you do manage to evade the guards, scale
the electric fences, dodge the landmines,

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:36.360
and avoid the automatic gun turrets, then
you’ll probably still end up as the meal

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:38.200
of some wild animal.

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:42.979
Due to the uninhabitable nature of the dangerous
no-man’s-land, the area has become something

00:07:42.979 --> 00:07:48.540
of a nature reserve, where some extremely
rare plants and animals have found their unlikely

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:49.540
home.

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:55.090
These endangered animals include the iconic
red-crowned crane, and the majestic—but

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:57.620
dangerous—Siberian Tiger.

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:03.260
The DMZ was created as an armistice agreement
to pacify the brutal Korean War.

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:08.759
Technically, both nations are still at war,
which is fairly unnerving since North Korea

00:08:08.759 --> 00:08:14.169
has since developed nuclear weapons and regularly
threatens to use them if provoked.

00:08:14.169 --> 00:08:17.580
That doesn’t mean everything has been peaceful
though.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:25.389
Between 1953 and 1999 around five hundred
South Koreans, fifty US troops and two hundred

00:08:25.389 --> 00:08:31.690
and fifty North Koreans have died in sporadic
outbreaks of conflict within the highly militarized

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:32.690
zone.

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:38.700
Of the around 50,000 people that have defected
from the North to the South, only a handful

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:42.349
have actually crossed the southern border,
through the DMZ.

00:08:42.349 --> 00:08:48.910
That goes to show just how fortified and dangerous
the border really is… and how well it works.

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:54.210
Back in medieval times, people used moats
and drawbridges to keep their annoying neighbors

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:55.210
away.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:59.930
But one abbey took the idea of a moat a bit
too far…

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:03.640
Mont Saint-Michel is a natural “island fortress.”

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:10.300
The outcrop was formed 525 million years ago,
during the Cambrian period, and is made from

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:12.350
volcanic Leucogranite.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:18.160
Over millions of years, the softer rock around
it was worn away to leave the now 92 meter

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:22.270
high outcrop, towering high above the surrounding
bay.

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:28.060
The abbey is tidally isolated, where at high
tide, the surrounding marshes, lowlands and

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:33.100
land bridges are swallowed up into the watery
depths, along with anything locked outside

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:34.100
the gates.

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:38.080
That could have been peasants who weren’t
permitted to enter, or invading armies trying

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:40.490
to claim the Mont for themselves.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:45.270
This natural process provided a fantastic
deterrent against a siege, since they usually

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:51.250
lasted days to months, but the tide meant
any army would only have a few hours to take

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:56.580
the fort, or they would lose their men, supplies,
and siege equipment to the sea.

00:09:56.580 --> 00:10:00.890
Even if you were determined enough to swim
up to the island to try and get access, you’re

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:05.300
probably better off waiting for the tides
and gates to lower, as Mont Saint Michel has

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:08.290
some pretty serious fortifications.

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:13.190
The entirety of the island is surrounded by
a large wall with battlements, ready to repel

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:14.190
invaders.

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:18.380
The streets and passageways up to the monastery
are very narrow and are flanked by elevated

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:22.940
walkways that are perfect for raining down
arrows or boiling oil.

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:26.740
Basically, if you are trying to get to the
top to check out the view, but you’re not

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:27.740
welcome…

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:30.029
you’re going to have a very bad time.

00:10:30.029 --> 00:10:35.350
Mont Saint-Michel remained unconquered during
the entirety of the Hundred Year War between

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:37.480
France and England.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:42.800
Even a small garrison fended off a full attack
by the English in 1433.

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:47.310
Several siege machines were abandoned by the
English during the failed assault as the tides

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:51.080
closed in, and can still be seen today.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:55.170
These days, there’s a bridge which gives
people access from the mainland even at high

00:10:55.170 --> 00:11:01.540
tide, but every 18 years or so, a “super
tide” comes along, and last year, 2015,

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:07.500
in March, it temporarily submerged part of
the bridge, making Mont Saint Michel a true

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:10.640
island fortress once more.

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:15.020
If you happen to find yourself surviving a
disaster that wipes out most of the planet’s

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:17.670
crops, there’s no need to worry about starving.

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:21.630
You just have to take a quick trip down to
your “local” doomsday seed vault and pick

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:23.339
up a packet or two.

00:11:23.339 --> 00:11:29.260
The Svalbard Global Seed Vault is so fortified
that it could survive the apocalypse... or

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:32.750
at least it should do, else the owners might
want their money back.

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:38.830
The vault was designed to outlast almost any
imaginable disaster ranging from extreme climate

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:41.660
change to a nuclear war.

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:47.240
Built on the Norwegian island of Spitsbergen
only 1300 kilometres from the North Pole,

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:53.130
the vault is about as isolated as a location
can be without being inaccessible.

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:58.480
If you manage to fight your way through the
local polar bears and reach the vault, you

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:03.010
will find a wedge-shaped hunk of concrete
sticking out of an icy mountain.

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:08.000
Get past the heavy security and front doors
and you enter a reinforced tunnel which burrows

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:11.399
120 metres into the solid rock.

00:12:11.399 --> 00:12:15.899
The Svalbard Global seed vault serves as a
highly secure backup for other seed vaults

00:12:15.899 --> 00:12:19.950
around the world that are in less fortified
locations.

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:24.620
The vault currently holds millions of seeds
from thousands of crop species across the

00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:31.040
world, secured in airtight aluminium packets
and refrigerated at negative eighteen degrees

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:32.330
Celsius.

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:36.450
While it’s not the comfiest of places to
spend your time, the conditions are perfect

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:40.200
for preserving most seed species indefinitely.

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:45.390
Because the facility was built in such a remote
location, with such frigid conditions; even

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:50.330
if the power fails, the temperature would
stabilize to about negative three degrees

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:55.880
Celsius, cold enough to keep some crucial
seeds preserved for decades.

00:12:55.880 --> 00:13:01.350
It may not be the most heavily guarded location
on the planet, but a vault that could potentially

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:07.460
outlast the rest of humanity definitely earns
the right to be on this list.

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:13.540
When a bunker used to be the North American
Aerospace Command Centre, as well as spearheading

00:13:13.540 --> 00:13:18.310
U.S Space Command missions, you know that
it’s going to have some serious hardware

00:13:18.310 --> 00:13:20.779
behind those heavy blast doors.

00:13:20.779 --> 00:13:26.740
Buried six hundred metres under a granite
mountain in Colorado Springs lies the Cheyenne

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:34.310
Mountain Complex, a massive military structure
built in 1967 by the US Government to be a

00:13:34.310 --> 00:13:36.120
central operations base.

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:40.560
The bunker was designed to withstand the most
powerful weapons ever created, and is able

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:45.959
to survive a thirty megaton nuclear detonation
within two kilometres, a blast that would

00:13:45.959 --> 00:13:50.300
completely flatten a large metropolis like
London or New York City.

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:52.490
That’s pretty impressive.

00:13:52.490 --> 00:13:57.529
The main access tunnel is lined with a series
of 25-ton blast doors, each with pressurized

00:13:57.529 --> 00:14:03.399
compartments designed to filter out any nuclear,
biological or chemical contaminants.

00:14:03.399 --> 00:14:08.200
At the height of its operation, these checkpoints
would have been heavily guarded, and only

00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:11.970
those with the highest military clearance
were able to pass.

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:17.149
The facility was fully self-contained, and
provided living quarters for round-the-clock

00:14:17.149 --> 00:14:18.700
operation.

00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:24.010
Everything the base needed was provided or
stored on site, with power generators, food

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:28.620
and water storage, as well as fuel reservoirs
ensuring that the base could still operate

00:14:28.620 --> 00:14:31.089
when cut off from the outside world.

00:14:31.089 --> 00:14:36.779
This level of secrecy and security brought
the base fame in pop culture, with conspiracy

00:14:36.779 --> 00:14:41.970
theories and television shows like Stargate
being based on the military complex.

00:14:41.970 --> 00:14:47.120
The base actually acknowledged this and installed
a seemingly “high security” door labelled

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:52.470
“Stargate Command” which led to a storage
room for cleaning equipment.

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:56.899
Cheyenne Mountain has since been retired from
its original command-center duties and is

00:14:56.899 --> 00:15:00.370
now reserved for training and commercial communications
use.

00:15:00.370 --> 00:15:05.870
Though, fully operational or not, I would
prefer to be standing inside the bunker in

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:10.550
an emergency than outside it, banging on the
blast doors trying to get in.

00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:15.990
I mentioned earlier that Fort Knox wasn’t
the world’s leading stockpile of gold…

00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:20.730
and that’s because that honour belongs to
The Federal Reserve Bank of New York.

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:25.920
It’s the largest Federal Reserve Bank in
America, and like the others, it holds an

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:29.810
incredible amount of cash, gold and other
valuables.

00:15:29.810 --> 00:15:36.660
Like most banks, there is a 24/7 quick response
tactical assault team on standby for any sign

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:38.720
that the bank is being robbed.

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:43.600
The armed guards in and around the building
go through tough training courses to spot

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:48.850
the telltale signs that give away someone
who might be there attempting to make a bigger

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:51.780
withdrawal than they’re entitled to.

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:56.990
Past all the security and down a few flights
of stairs lies the gold vault, protected by

00:15:56.990 --> 00:16:00.230
a massive steel door with a multiple lock
mechanism.

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:03.540
People aren’t even allowed inside the vault
itself.

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:07.880
All interaction and movement of gold is done
by robots carrying pallets.

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:12.329
I guess robots are the only thing that wouldn’t
get gold fever being surrounded by tons of

00:16:12.329 --> 00:16:14.190
shiny yellow metal.

00:16:14.190 --> 00:16:19.510
The vault isn’t just buried underground,
but dug into the very bedrock of lower Manhattan.

00:16:19.510 --> 00:16:23.510
It actually sits at 80 feet below the street
level.

00:16:23.510 --> 00:16:27.510
So if you plan on digging your way in, it’s
going to take a long… long time.

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:31.970
But that’s assuming one of the marksmen
that patrol the perimeter of the building

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:35.570
doesn’t spot you before you even break through
the concrete.

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:40.930
With the armed guards, security checks, the
heavy vault doors and rock foundations, I’m

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:44.940
afraid the only way you’re going to see
any gold, is if you own it.

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:50.579
The vault contained seven thousand tons of
gold as of the last official inventory in

00:16:50.579 --> 00:16:51.579
2011.

00:16:51.579 --> 00:16:55.780
That’s far more than reported anywhere else
in the world.

00:16:55.780 --> 00:17:00.490
So if somebody does ever manage the impossible
and break into that vault, they’re going

00:17:00.490 --> 00:17:03.630
to have a tough time carrying it out.

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:09.390
When you picture a heavily fortified location,
you probably think about bunkers, or vaults,

00:17:09.390 --> 00:17:13.589
or even your wallet when it’s your turn
to get the next round in…but probably not

00:17:13.589 --> 00:17:14.610
boats.

00:17:14.610 --> 00:17:18.080
Well, this isn’t any old boat.

00:17:18.080 --> 00:17:19.850
The USS George H.W.

00:17:19.850 --> 00:17:25.110
Bush is arguably the most fortified military
position on the planet, despite…

00:17:25.110 --> 00:17:27.510
well… not actually staying one place.

00:17:27.510 --> 00:17:33.210
It’s one of ten Nimitz class Supercarriers
in the United States Navy, and as well as

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:38.230
being almost impervious to attack, it’s
one of the most formidable sights for an enemy

00:17:38.230 --> 00:17:39.270
to see.

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:43.401
You sure as hell don’t want to be the reason
why it’s arrived armed and ready for action.

00:17:43.401 --> 00:17:49.000
At three hundred and thirty-two metres long
and weighing around one hundred thousand tons

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:53.230
when fully loaded, it’s no surprise that
its presence means business.

00:17:53.230 --> 00:17:59.140
It’s capable of carrying 90 aircraft, including
helicopters, and can launch one every twenty

00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:02.940
seconds on offensive or defensive missions.

00:18:02.940 --> 00:18:08.960
Capable of repelling entire navies of some
countries by itself, a U.S Carrier Strike

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:13.910
Group, which works with the aircraft carrier,
has multiple layers of state-of-the-art defense.

00:18:13.910 --> 00:18:18.720
Dedicated Cruisers, Destroyers, and Frigates
work together to defend the carrier from attack

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:23.590
by air and sea, whether the assailants are
other ships or submarines.

00:18:23.590 --> 00:18:28.179
The group works closely with advanced radar
systems and electronic countermeasures to

00:18:28.179 --> 00:18:33.920
spot enemies long before they are in range,
and interfere with their capability to fight.

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:39.200
The aircraft carrier itself has an array of
weaponry; from a number of surface-to-air

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:44.419
missile launchers that target enemy aircraft,
to Phalanx weapons systems which lock on to

00:18:44.419 --> 00:18:48.670
incoming missiles and shred them in a hail
of bullets.

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:53.280
Even if something does make it all the way
through the battlegroup, the swarms of advanced

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:57.440
aircraft, and the carrier’s point-defense
guns, you would still need to get through

00:18:57.440 --> 00:19:02.920
the two and a half inch thick Kevlar armor
which protects the vital parts of the ship.

00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:07.490
So yeah… unless you have a large army with
some sophisticated weaponry, you probably

00:19:07.490 --> 00:19:09.160
aren’t going to land a scratch.

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:13.490
There’s a fortified place for almost every
situation, whether it’s surviving the end

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:17.040
of the world, hoarding your treasure, or repelling
the British.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:19.230
Thanks for the view, subscribe for more Thoughty2

